<p class="lt-chem-151883">In <a title="13.2: Chemical Kinetics" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/13%3A_Kinetic_Methods/13.02%3A_Chemical_Kinetics#example13.2.1" rel="internal">Example 13.2.1
</a> we determine the analyte&rsquo;s initial concentration by measuring the amount of analyte that has not reacted. Sometimes it is more convenient to measure the concentration of a reagent that reacts with the analyte, or to measure the concentration of one of the reaction&rsquo;s products. We can use a one-point fixed-time integral method if we know the reaction&rsquo;s stoichiometry. For example, if we measure the concentration of the product, <em>P</em>, in the reaction</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">\[A+R \rightarrow P \nonumber\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">then the concentration of the analyte at time <em>t </em>is</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883"><a name="equation13.2.12"></a>\[[A]_{t}=[A]_{0}-[P]_{t} \label{13.12}\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">because the stoichiometry between the analyte and product is 1:1. If the reaction is pseudo-first-order in <em>A</em>, then substituting Equation \ref{13.12} into Equation \ref{13.7} gives</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883"><a name="equation13.2.13"></a>\[\ln \left([A]_{0}-[P]_{t}\right) = \ln{[A]_{0}} - k^{\prime} t \label{13.13}\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">which we simplify by writing in exponential form.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883"><a name="equation13.2.14"></a>\[[A]_0 - [P]_t = [A]_0 e^{-k^{\prime}t} \label{13.14}\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">Finally, solving Equation \ref{13.14} for [<em>A</em>]<sub>0 </sub>gives the following equation.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883"><a name="equation13.2.15"></a>\[[A]_{0}=\frac{[P]_{t}}{1-e^{-k^{\prime}t}} \label{13.15}\]</p>

<section class="box-example">
<header>
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/13:_Kinetic_Methods/13.02:_Chemical_Kinetics" class="mt-section"><span id="Example_13.2.2"></span><h5 id="Example_13.2.2-151883"><span class="lt-icon-default">Example 13.2.2
</span></h5>
</div></header>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">The concentration of thiocyanate, SCN<sup>&ndash;</sup>, is determined from the pseudo-first-order kinetics of its reaction with excess Fe<sup>3</sup><sup>+</sup> to form a reddish-colored complex of Fe(SCN)<sup>2+</sup>. The reaction&rsquo;s progress is monitored by measuring the absorbance of Fe(SCN)<sup>2+</sup> at a wavelength of 480 nm. When using a standard solution of 0.100 M SCN<sup>&ndash;</sup>, the concentration of Fe(SCN)<sup>2+</sup> after 10 s is 0.0516 M. The concentration of Fe(SCN)<sup>2+</sup> in a sample that contains an unknown amount of SCN<sup>&ndash;</sup> is 0.0420 M after 10 s. What is the initial concentration of SCN<sup>&ndash;</sup> in the sample?</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883"><strong>Solution</strong></p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">First, we must determine a value for the pseudo-first-order rate constant, \(k^{\prime}\). Using Equation \ref{13.13}, we find that its value is</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">\[k^{\prime} = \frac{\ln{[A]_{0}} - \ln \left([A]_{0} - [P]_{1}\right)}{t}= \frac {\ln(0.100) - \ln(0.100 - 0.0516)} {10.0 \text{ s}} = 0.0726 \text{ s}^{-1} \nonumber\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">Next, we use Equation \ref{13.15} to determine the initial concentration of SCN<sup>&ndash;</sup> in the sample.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-151883">\[[A]_{0}=\frac{[P]_{t}}{1-e^{-k^{\prime} t}}=\frac{0.0420 \mathrm{M}}{1-e^{-\left(0.0726 \text{ s}^{-1}\right)(10.0 \text{ s})}}=0.0868 \mathrm{M} \nonumber\]</p>
</section>

